# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for pto-core.
#
# SCOPE DISCIPLINE
# ----------------
# This file may only suppress reports whose every frame is in code we neither
# wrote nor build with instrumentation. It must never be used to silence a
# report that touches modules/*/src or modules/*/include -- if a first-party
# race shows up, fix the race.
#
# That rule has already paid for itself once: the first containerised run
# reported a data race in hnswlib::getRandomLevel reached through
# scrna::hnsw_build_and_search. It would have been trivially "suppressible" as
# third-party. It was a real unsynchronised read-modify-write of a shared PRNG,
# and it was fixed instead (see third_party/hnswlib/VERSION.txt).
#
# Every entry below states what it covers, why it is not a first-party defect,
# and what evidence backs that.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. libomp's internal lock pool.  FALSE POSITIVE.
#
# Reports of the shape:
#     Atomic read of size 1 ... pthread_mutex_lock  <- interceptor
#       #1 (libomp.so.5+0xb7064)
#     Previous write of size 1 ... pthread_mutex_init
#       #1 (libomp.so.5+0xb3aa8)
#     Location is heap block ... allocated by (libomp.so.5+0x225f8)
#
# Every frame is either libomp or a TSan interceptor; none is ours. libomp is
# linked uninstrumented from the distro package, so TSan cannot see its internal
# happens-before edges even with libarcher loaded (archer annotates OpenMP
# *semantics* -- barriers, taskwait, reductions -- not the runtime's own lock
# pool bootstrap).
#
# Evidence this is not first-party: rebuilding with -DSCRNA_ENABLE_HNSW=OFF
# leaves these reports unchanged in shape and origin, and they persist with a
# body that touches no user locks at all.
#
# `called_from_lib` and NOT `race:libomp.so` / `race:__kmp_`. Those two were
# tried first and are catastrophically over-broad: TSan matches `race:` against
# ANY frame in the stack, and `__kmp_invoke_microtask` appears in the stack of
# every race inside an OpenMP parallel region -- including first-party ones.
# A negative control caught it: with the hnswlib getRandomLevel fix deliberately
# reverted, the known data race was reported ZERO times under those rules. The
# suppression file had blinded the entire harness. `called_from_lib` instead
# matches only when the *caller* of the intercepted function lives in libomp,
# which is true for the runtime's own lock pool and false for our code.
called_from_lib:libomp.so

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. hnswlib's per-element lock array.  GENUINE, UPSTREAM, NOT OURS.
#
# TSan's deadlock detector reports thousands of lock-order inversions between
# hnswlib's `link_list_locks_[cur_c]` (taken in addPoint, hnswalg.h:1185) and
# `label_op_locks_[...]` / `link_list_locks_[neighbour]` (taken in
# mutuallyConnectNewElement, hnswalg.h:529,555).
#
# This is NOT labelled a false positive. Two threads inserting elements whose
# neighbour sets overlap can acquire the same pair of per-element mutexes in
# opposite orders, which is a real lock-order inversion and therefore a real
# potential deadlock in upstream hnswlib's design. It is suppressed here rather
# than fixed because fixing it means restructuring hnswlib's locking, which is
# a change to vendored upstream code with a far worse risk profile than the
# residual, and because it is not observed to deadlock in practice at the
# concurrency this library uses.
#
# Attribution is exact, not assumed: building with -DSCRNA_ENABLE_HNSW=OFF
# drops the count from 7,560 across six runs to ZERO, while first-party
# parallel code (parallel_for_checked, the brute-force k-NN region) is
# unchanged. Our parallel regions take no std::mutex at all -- only one named
# `#pragma omp critical` -- so a lock-order inversion cannot originate there.
#
# Tracked in third_party/hnswlib/VERSION.txt. Revisit on any hnswlib bump.
deadlock:hnswalg.h
deadlock:hnswlib
deadlock:libomp.so

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Uninstrumented distro shared objects reached from cuttag_profiler and
#    fastq_stream (whole-tree runs only; TSAN_MODULES=scrna_matrix skips them).
#
# htslib is a large C codebase linked uninstrumented. cuttag_profiler
# serialises all htslib access per BamReader, which the bam_query suite covers.
# zlib/libdeflate likewise.
called_from_lib:libhts.so
called_from_lib:libz.so
called_from_lib:libdeflate.so

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. genomic_toolkit's FRiP reduction.  FALSE POSITIVE -- DELIBERATELY NOT
#    SUPPRESSED. Documented here so it is re-verified, not re-investigated.
#
# On a host whose libomp is not TSan-instrumented (Homebrew's is not, and
# neither is any macOS toolchain), running `genomic_toolkit frip` under TSan
# reports 20-40 races of this shape:
#
#     WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
#       Read of size 8 ... FripCounter::add_batch (.omp_outlined_debug__
#                          .omp.reduction.reduction_func) frip.hpp:106
#         #1 __kmp_hyper_barrier_gather (libomp.dylib)
#       Previous write ... FripCounter::add_batch (.omp_outlined) frip.hpp:105
#       Location is stack of thread T3.
#
# No entry is added for it, because the scope rule at the top of this file
# forbids one: the reported frames are in modules/genomic_toolkit/include/
# toolkit/frip.hpp, which is first-party. A `race:frip.hpp` rule would also
# blind the harness to any genuine race later introduced into that file --
# exactly the failure mode the negative control in section 1 caught.
#
# It is a false positive, on four independent pieces of evidence:
#
#   1. The reported location is "stack of thread T<n>" -- the private per-thread
#      reduction copies libomp creates, not shared state. The loop's only write
#      to shared memory is `f.flags |= kInPeak` on data[i], and the schedule is
#      static over distinct i, so no two threads touch the same Fragment.
#   2. The non-libomp frames are the compiler-generated `.omp_outlined` and
#      `.omp.reduction.reduction_func` thunks, reached from
#      __kmp_hyper_barrier_gather. TSan cannot see the barrier's happens-before
#      edges through an uninstrumented libomp, so it reports the reduction
#      combine itself.
#   3. OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 under TSan reports ZERO races: with no combine step
#      there is nothing for TSan to misread.
#   4. Rebuilding the same source with -DCMAKE_DISABLE_FIND_PACKAGE_OpenMP=ON
#      under TSan reports ZERO races AND produces byte-identical output.
#
# Correctness was established separately from the sanitizer: 55 runs of the
# same 200k-fragment workload across 11 thread counts (1..128), without TSan,
# produced exactly ONE distinct JSON result. A broken `reduction(+:...)` drifts;
# this does not.
#
# To get a clean TSan run over the FRiP path, either build libomp with
# -DLIBOMP_TSAN_SUPPORT=ON and load libarcher, or configure with
# -DCMAKE_DISABLE_FIND_PACKAGE_OpenMP=ON -- the reduction is the only OpenMP in
# the module and disabling it changes no results.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. The peaks/fastq_stream "write failure" CLI tests.  NOT A SUPPRESSION --
#    the two assertions skip themselves under TSan; documented here because
#    the mechanism is the same class of finding as sections 1/2/4.
#
# modules/peaks/scripts/test_cli.sh ("a narrowPeak write failure exits 1") and
# modules/fastq_stream/tests/test_cli.sh ("JSON report write failure") both
# impose `ulimit -f 1` (512 bytes) with SIGXFSZ ignored, to prove the tool
# reports EFBIG cleanly instead of exiting 0 over a truncated write. Under a
# TSan build, that limit kills the process before main runs: TSan's own
# runtime needs somewhere between 128 KiB and 512 KiB of file-size headroom
# just to initialize, so it SIGBUSes inside its own strlen interceptor during
# the first std::string construction (Options::Options(), before argument
# parsing) rather than reaching the code path under test. Confirmed by
# sweeping the limit from 512 B to 512 KiB against a debug build under gdb --
# the crash is entirely inside __tsan::MemoryAccessRangeT, no application
# frame involved, and it clears once the limit is raised, independent of
# anything either test does.
#
# Not suppressible here: this is a DEADLYSIGNAL, not a data race or lock-order
# inversion, so it has no frame for a race:/deadlock: rule to match. And
# raising the limit enough to clear TSan's floor also clears the few KiB
# either fixture actually writes, which would stop the assertion from ever
# exercising EFBIG -- there is no limit that is simultaneously safe for TSan's
# runtime and tight enough for either fixture without inflating it to tens of
# thousands of records, a real cost to a CLI smoke test under TSan's overhead
# for no corresponding coverage gained. Both scripts detect TSAN_OPTIONS (set
# by scripts/run_tsan.sh for every invocation) and skip loudly with this
# reasoning inline rather than silently passing or failing red for a
# limitation in the tool, not the code under test.
